Author: Martin Sedlacek <martin.martin.sedlacek@gmail.com>
Date:   Fri Aug 14 01:44:05 2026 +0200

    Give OmniSurfaceMaterialPrim.preset_name a default
    
    OmniSurfaceMaterialPrim.__init__ took preset_name as a required positional, but
    the class is written throughout for it to be optional: the docstring says "If
    None, defaults to OmniSurface", and both readers branch on it --
    
        mdl_name: "OmniSurfacePresets.mdl" if self.preset_name else "OmniSurface.mdl"
        mtl_name: f"OmniSurface_{self.preset_name}" if self.preset_name else "OmniSurface"
    
    MaterialPrim.get_material() cannot supply one and does not try. It picks the
    subclass by matching an EXISTING prim's mdl asset against supports_material(),
    which accepts plain OmniSurface.mdl / "OmniSurface" as well as the presets, and
    then calls material_class(relative_prim_path=..., name=..., **kwargs) with no
    preset in kwargs. Every non-particle-system OmniSurface material therefore raised
    
        TypeError: missing a required argument: 'preset_name'
    
    The only caller that has a preset -- MicroPhysicalParticleSystem._get_particle_
    material_template -- passes it explicitly and is unaffected.
    
    Nothing can regress from the default: mdl_name and mtl_name are read only by
    MaterialPrim._load(), and BasePrim.load() calls _load() only when the prim does
    not already exist. The get_material inference path only ever wraps a material
    that is already on the stage, so on that path neither property is evaluated.
    
    Found via REALM, whose drawer tasks (open_drawer, close_drawer) load
    custom_assets/impact_drawer/usd/cabinet.usd and could not build at all, single-env
    or vectorized.
    
    Verified with tests/test_vector_integrity.py --cells 8:Default,9:Default
    --num_envs 2: both cells now get past the material entirely and log
    "Imported scene 0". They still do not build, but the blocker has moved to an
    unrelated assert on the same asset's authored transform --
    
      omnigibson/prims/xform_prim.py:153 in _set_xform_properties
      AssertionError: /World/scene_0/drawer:
        old_pos tensor([0.0243, 0.8223, -0.0317])
        new_pos tensor([2.3468e-04, 8.1015e-01, -6.3757e-02])
        old_orn tensor([0.0007, 0.7068, 0.7073, 0.0102])
        new_orn tensor([0.7071, -0.0109, -0.0109, 0.7070])
    
    -- where the method rewrites the xformOp order to [translate, orient, scale],
    writes back the pose it just read, re-reads it and requires the two to agree. The
    two quaternions carry the same component magnitudes in a different order. Not
    addressed here.

